PR #11 — Image ingestion: decode, EXIF orientation, downscale
Branch: feat/image-ingestion           Depends on: #10               Status: TODO
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Turning whatever the user gives us into a normalised bitmap. More failure
  modes live here than anywhere else in the product.

IMPLEMENTS
  [ ] File validation (type, size, dimensions) before decode
  [ ] EXIF parse; apply orientation; strip EXIF from output
  [ ] Downscale to the analysis working size, keep the original for output
  [ ] Typed decode-failure reasons with user-facing guidance

EDGE CASES
  [ ] HEIC/HEIF — the default iPhone format, and browsers cannot decode it.
      Either ship a WASM decoder or detect and give exact conversion steps.
      Getting this wrong loses a large share of the target audience.
  [ ] All eight EXIF orientation values, including the mirrored ones
  [ ] Images with no EXIF at all
  [ ] CMYK JPEGs, 16-bit PNGs, images with alpha, progressive JPEGs
  [ ] Animated GIF/WebP — take frame one
  [ ] Dimensions beyond the browser's canvas limit (~16,384px)
  [ ] Corrupt, truncated or zero-byte files
  [ ] Extension lying about content — sniff the magic bytes
  [ ] Colour profiles: convert to sRGB or measured colours will be wrong
  [ ] Memory spikes on 48MP phone photos
  [ ] EXIF capture date used for the recency hint; absent on screenshots

SEO  n/a

TESTS
